.TH mWand 1
.SH "NAME"
\fBmwand\fP \(em motifWand, an application launcher, workspace switcher and
clock for motifWizard
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBmwand\fP [\fI-rcfile\fP \fBfile\fP] [\fI-title\fP \fBtitle\fP]
[\fI-hotkey\fP \fBkeysym\fP] [\fI-horizontal\fP | \fI+horizontal\fP]
[\fI-version\fP]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBmotifWand\fP, abbreviated \fBmWand\fP and invoked as \fBmwand\fP, is a small
panel holding user defined launcher menus, a workspace
switcher, a clock, and optionally a menu of session commands\&. It is an
optional companion to \fBmwizard\fP(1) and is built and installed separately\&.
.PP
The workspace switcher and the "occupy all workspaces" behavior use standard
EWMH properties, so \fBmwand\fP works with any window manager that implements
them\&. It speaks no private protocol to \fBmwizard\fP and needs no session
manager\&.
.SS "Options"
.IP "\fI-rcfile\fP\ \fBfile\fP" 10
Read configuration from \fBfile\fP instead of searching the usual locations\&.
.IP "\fI-title\fP\ \fBtitle\fP" 10
Set the window title\&. Defaults to \fIuser\fP@\fIhost\fP\&.
.IP "\fI-hotkey\fP\ \fBkeysym\fP" 10
Grab this key to raise and focus \fBmwand\fP\&.
.IP "\fI-horizontal\fP, \fI+horizontal\fP" 10
Force horizontal or vertical layout\&.
.IP "\fI-version\fP" 10
Print version information and exit\&.
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
.PP
\fBmwand\fP is configured from one file, searched for in this order:
.PP
.nf
$HOME/\&.mwandrc
$RCDIR/system\&.mwandrc
.fi
.PP
That file holds both the launcher menus and, in a \fBSettings\fP block, every
behavior setting\&. See \fBdoc/MWAND\&.md\fP for the full reference\&.
.PP
Unlike \fBxmtoolbox\fP, from which \fBmwand\fP derives, behavior is not read
from the X resource database\&. Neither is appearance: fonts and colors come
from the style file, \fB$HOME/\&.mstylesrc\fP, which \fBmwizard\fP(1) reads as
well so that the panel and the window manager look like one thing\&. See
\fBdoc/STYLE\&.md\fP\&. A setting left in \fB\&.Xdefaults\fP has no effect,
because both files are merged over it\&.
.PP
Versions before 1\&.2 read appearance from an app-defaults file installed as
\fBapp-defaults/MWand\fP\&. It is gone\&. Three shell resources are still read
from the X database, because Xt fetches them before any file is opened:
\fIMWand\&.x\fP, \fIMWand\&.y\fP and \fIMWand\&.mwmDecorations\fP\&.
.PP
The one exception is \fIrcFile\fP, which names the file the settings live in
and so must come from the X database or from \fI-rcfile\fP\&.
.SS "Settings"
.IP "\fItitle\fP" 10
Window title\&. Defaults to \fBmWand\fP\&. xmtoolbox titled the window
\fIuser\fP@\fIhost\fP, which a narrow panel clips; that string is a line in
the panel now -- see \fIuserHostDisplay\fP\&.
.IP "\fIhorizontal\fP" 10
Lay out horizontally rather than vertically\&. Default \fBFalse\fP\&.
.IP "\fIseparators\fP" 10
Draw separators between the parts\&. Default \fBTrue\fP\&.
.IP "\fIworkspaceSwitcher\fP" 10
Show the workspace switcher\&. Default \fBTrue\fP\&. It is hidden regardless
when there is only one workspace\&.
.IP "\fIdateTimeDisplay\fP" 10
Show the clock\&. Default \fBTrue\fP\&.
.IP "\fIdateTimeFormat\fP" 10
Clock format, as for \fBstrftime\fP(3)\&. Default \fB"%m/%d %l:%M %p"\fP\&.
.IP "\fIuserHostDisplay\fP" 10
Show who is logged in and where, as the last line of the panel\&. Default
\fBTrue\fP\&. The host name is shortened to its first component, so
\fBbox\&.example\&.org\fP shows as \fBbox\fP\&. If the system will not name
the host the login is shown alone, and if it will not name either the line is
left out\&.
.IP "\fIoccupyAllWorkspaces\fP" 10
Ask the window manager to show \fBmwand\fP on every workspace\&. Default
\fBTrue\fP\&.
.IP "\fIoccupyAllMonitors\fP" 10
Ask the window manager to show \fBmwand\fP on every monitor\&. Default
\fBFalse\fP, which keeps it on the primary monitor \(em with mWizard\'s
\fIperMonitorWorkspaces\fP on, \fBTrue\fP is what keeps the panel on the
screen whatever workspace any monitor happens to be showing\&. Requires
mWizard 1\&.3\&.
.IP "\fIhotkey\fP" 10
Key that raises and focuses \fBmwand\fP\&. Unset by default\&.
.IP "\fIsessionMenu\fP" 10
Include the session actions in the command menu\&. Default \fBTrue\fP\&. With
this off the menu keeps \fBExecute\&.\&.\&.\fP and is labelled \fBCommands\fP
rather than \fBSession\fP\&.
.IP "\fIlockCommand\fP, \fIlogoutCommand\fP, \fIsuspendCommand\fP, \fIrebootCommand\fP, \fIshutdownCommand\fP" 10
Commands the session menu runs\&. An entry whose command is empty is not shown
at all\&. \fIlogoutCommand\fP, \fIrebootCommand\fP and \fIshutdownCommand\fP
ask for confirmation first\&. The last three default to \fBsystemctl\fP
invocations; the first two are empty\&.
.SS "Menus"
.PP
A top level menu is a title followed by a brace block\&. \fB&\fP marks the
keyboard mnemonic, a colon separates an item's title from its command, and
\fBSEPARATOR\fP places a separator\&. Menus may nest\&.
.PP
\fBMWINFO\fP and \fBMONITORS\fP are the items that are not commands\&. They
post \fBmWinfo\fP, the window manager's About window, and \fBmWmonitor\fP,
its monitor arranger, and are written bare like \fBSEPARATOR\fP:
\fBmwand\fP supplies the labels and asks the window manager for the windows
over the same path its own command menu uses\&. Either may appear in any menu
at any depth\&. \fBMONITORS\fP requires mWizard 1\&.3\&.
.PP
.nf
&Programs
{
    &Terminal: xterm
    SEPARATOR
    MONITORS
    MWINFO
    SEPARATOR
    &More
    {
        Ca&lculator: xcalc
    }
}
.fi
.PP
Command strings may contain environment variables in \fBsh\fP(1) syntax\&.
Lines beginning with \fB#\fP or \fB!\fP are comments\&.
.PP
A top level menu titled exactly \fBSettings\fP would be mistaken for the
settings block\&. Write it with a mnemonic, \fB&Settings\fP, or give it any
other title\&.
.SH "SIGNALS"
.IP "\fBSIGUSR1\fP" 10
Re-read the configuration file\&. On a parse error the previous configuration
stays active and the error is reported\&.
.SH "WINDOW MANAGER UTILITIES"
.PP
The Commands menu carries two items that belong to the window manager rather
than to \fBmWand\fP: \fBExecute\&.\&.\&.\fP, a prompt for a command to run,
and \fBAbout mWizard\&.\&.\&.\fP, which posts \fBmWinfo\fP\&. Neither is
configurable and neither can be missing: \fBmWand\fP finds the window manager
through \fB_NET_SUPPORTING_WM_CHECK\fP and \fB_NET_WM_PID\fP and signals it,
sending \fBSIGUSR1\fP for the first and \fBSIGUSR2\fP for the second\&. Under
a window manager that publishes neither property, or that does not provide
these windows, the item says so\&.
.SH "FILES"
.PP
.nf
$HOME/\&.mwandrc
$RCDIR/system\&.mwandrc
$HOME/\&.mstylesrc
$RCDIR/system\&.mstylesrc
.fi
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.PP
\fBMSTYLESRC\fP, if set, names the style file to read instead of
\fB$HOME/\&.mstylesrc\fP\&. \fBmwizard\fP(1) honours it too, so one setting
restyles both\&.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
\fBmwizard\fP(1), \fBmwizardrc\fP(4), \fBstrftime\fP(3)
.PP
\fBdoc/MWAND\&.md\fP and \fBdoc/STYLE\&.md\fP describe the rc file and the
style file in full\&.
